# Build context exclusions.
#
# The rule: anything not needed to BUILD the image, and anything that would make the
# build context large enough to be slow or leak something, is excluded. Docker sends
# the whole context to the daemon before the first instruction runs, so a missing
# entry here costs seconds on every build even when no layer changes.

# Dependencies: reinstalled inside the image by `npm ci`. Copying the host's
# node_modules is the classic mistake - it is built for the host's platform and
# its Node ABI, so it either breaks in the image or silently pins the image to
# whatever the developer happened to have installed.
node_modules

# Build output: rebuilt inside the image. Copying the host's dist would make the
# image a wrapper around whatever was on the machine rather than a build of the
# source that is actually in the context.
dist
coverage
.data
*.tsbuildinfo

# Load-suite artifacts and logs. Generated, not source, and the metrics files are
# large enough to notice in the transfer size.
docs/benchmarks
docs/load-artifacts
*.log
.smoke.*

# k6 binary, downloaded into the repository and ~100 MB.
.tools

# Version control and CI. .git alone can be hundreds of megabytes, and the CI
# directory describes how to test the image rather than being part of it.
.git
.gitignore
.gitattributes
.github
.claude

# Docker's own files, which would otherwise be copied into the image they define.
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
docker-compose.yml

# Documentation. Not needed to build or run, and the deployment notes would
# otherwise need to stay accurate about paths inside the image.
# ADRs and NOTES are kept out for the same reason: they are for readers, not the
# runtime.
docs
*.md
LICENSE

# Editor and OS noise.
.vscode
.idea
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# The template is not a secret, but nothing inside the image reads it, and a
# future version of this file might be pasted into rather than left empty.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Local tooling config that affects development only.
.eslintcache
.prettierignore
.editorconfig
